## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

### Summary

| Severity | Count |
|----------|-------|
| **Critical** | **2** |
| **Medium** | **2** |
| **Low** | **1** |

This suite is a textbook **coverage-touching** test class. All 10 public methods on `ProductCatalog` are called — achieving high line coverage — but almost nothing is actually verified. 6 of 10 tests have **zero assertions**, and the remaining 4 only assert `IsNotNull`, which verifies the return type, not the behavior. This explains exactly why bugs escape to production.

---

### 🔴 Critical Findings

#### 1. Coverage-Touching Pattern (systemic — entire file)

Every public method is called in declaration order with no meaningful verification. This is the #1 reason bugs ship despite 95% coverage.

**Tests with zero assertions (6 of 10):**

| Line | Method | What it misses |
|------|--------|---------------|
| 17 | `AddProduct_Works` | Never checks the product was actually stored |
| 33 | `UpdatePrice_Works` | Never checks the price changed to 14.99 |
| 39 | `RemoveProduct_Works` | Never checks the product is gone |
| 64 | `GetProductCount_Works` | Never checks the count value |
| 69 | `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` | Never checks prices were reduced |
| 85 | `ImportFromCsv_Works` | Never checks a product was imported |

**Fix example — `UpdatePrice_Works` (line 33):**
```csharp
// BEFORE — passes even if UpdatePrice is a no-op
_catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);

// AFTER — verifies the actual behavior
_catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
var updated = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, updated!.Price);
```

**Fix example — `RemoveProduct_Works` (line 39):**
```csharp
// BEFORE
_catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");

// AFTER
_catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));
```

**Fix example — `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (line 69):**
```csharp
// BEFORE
_catalog.ApplyBulkDiscount(0.10m);

// AFTER
_catalog.ApplyBulkDiscount(0.10m);
Assert.AreEqual(8.991m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001")!.Price);
Assert.AreEqual(17.991m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-002")!.Price);
```

#### 2. `IsNotNull`-Only Assertions (lines 28, 51, 59, 81)

`GetProduct_Works`, `SearchProducts_Works`, `GetAllProducts_Works`, and `ExportToCsv_Works` assert only `IsNotNull`. Since these methods return `List<T>` or non-nullable types, `IsNotNull` can never fail in normal operation. The actual content is never checked.

**Fix example — `SearchProducts_Works` (line 46):**
```csharp
// BEFORE — passes even if Search returns wrong results
